1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,997
These yellow crystals are on Mars.

2
00:00:02,148 --> 00:00:03,808
Nobody staged this picture.

3
00:00:03,924 --> 00:00:09,044
A rover drove over a rock, the rock
cracked open, and this was inside.

4
00:00:09,427 --> 00:00:12,875
It is pure sulfur,
the first ever found on Mars.

5
00:00:13,084 --> 00:00:16,904
And according to the mission's own
project scientist, it shouldn't be there.

6
00:00:17,229 --> 00:00:18,518
So how did it get there?

7
00:00:18,959 --> 00:00:21,374
Scientists now have a serious answer.

8
00:00:21,699 --> 00:00:23,534
But they don't all agree on it.

9
00:00:24,045 --> 00:00:25,821
May thirtieth, twenty twenty-four.

10
00:00:26,099 --> 00:00:29,559
NASA's Curiosity rover, a car-sized robot,

11
00:00:29,675 --> 00:00:33,030
is climbing a valley on the side
of a mountain on Mars.

12
00:00:33,355 --> 00:00:35,735
The mountain is called Mount Sharp.

13
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:41,273
It rises about three miles, five kilometers,
from the floor of Gale crater.

14
00:00:41,877 --> 00:00:46,103
Curiosity has been driving up
its lower slopes since twenty fourteen,

15
00:00:46,277 --> 00:00:49,028
reading the layers like
pages in a history book.

16
00:00:49,539 --> 00:00:52,372
The team was not looking
for sulfur that day.

17
00:00:52,755 --> 00:00:54,915
The rover was simply driving.

18
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:59,895
One of its aluminum wheels rolled
over a pale rock, and the rock broke apart.

19
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:02,275
There is no video of that moment.

20
00:01:02,972 --> 00:01:07,058
What exists are the photos
the rover took in the days after.

21
00:01:07,662 --> 00:01:12,817
A few days later, the camera on the end
of the robotic arm moved in close.

22
00:01:13,328 --> 00:01:17,113
Yellow crystals, hidden
under a reddish, dusty crust.

23
00:01:17,554 --> 00:01:22,801
In this view from the mast camera, the patch
of broken pieces is about five inches,

24
00:01:22,917 --> 00:01:24,984
thirteen centimeters, across.

25
00:01:25,228 --> 00:01:27,387
But yellow doesn't prove anything.

26
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:29,338
Plenty of minerals are yellow.

27
00:01:29,779 --> 00:01:34,342
So the team used another
instrument on the same arm: APXS,

28
00:01:34,586 --> 00:01:37,105
the Alpha Particle X-ray Spectrometer.

29
00:01:37,546 --> 00:01:41,447
It holds a small radioactive
source against the rock and reads

30
00:01:41,505 --> 00:01:43,398
the X-rays that come back.

31
00:01:43,839 --> 00:01:46,707
Every element answers
with its own signature.

32
00:01:47,218 --> 00:01:49,772
This material answered as sulfur.

33
00:01:50,155 --> 00:01:52,686
Not sulfur locked inside another mineral.

34
00:01:53,127 --> 00:01:55,658
Elemental sulfur, on its own.

35
00:01:56,610 --> 00:02:01,184
It's the same yellow substance you
can find around volcanic vents on Earth.

36
00:02:01,788 --> 00:02:04,911
Now, sulfur on Mars is not news.

37
00:02:05,155 --> 00:02:08,743
Curiosity had spent months
in a region rich in sulfates:

38
00:02:09,068 --> 00:02:14,942
minerals where sulfur is bound to oxygen
and to metals like calcium and magnesium.

39
00:02:15,383 --> 00:02:20,132
They are thought to have formed
as water evaporated, long ago.

40
00:02:20,735 --> 00:02:22,535
Pure sulfur is something else.

41
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:25,960
It only forms under a narrow
range of conditions.

42
00:02:26,401 --> 00:02:30,941
On Earth, we usually find it
near volcanoes and hot springs.

43
00:02:31,266 --> 00:02:35,724
And around this part of Mount Sharp,
there is no evidence of either.

44
00:02:36,235 --> 00:02:40,995
Curiosity's project scientist,
Ashwin Vasavada, put it plainly.

45
00:02:41,204 --> 00:02:42,469
It shouldn't be there.

46
00:02:42,911 --> 00:02:44,606
So now we have to explain it.

47
00:02:45,047 --> 00:02:47,101
And it wasn't just one rock.

48
00:02:47,543 --> 00:02:49,632
Look at the floor of the channel.

49
00:02:49,841 --> 00:02:54,358
Those bright stones scattered
across it are the same kind of rock.

50
00:02:54,683 --> 00:02:58,050
Close up, their outsides
are white and full of holes.

51
00:02:58,224 --> 00:03:00,372
Inside, they are sulfur.

52
00:03:00,373 --> 00:03:01,882
Here is the strange part.

53
00:03:02,393 --> 00:03:05,458
From orbit, this patch
looked unremarkable.

54
00:03:05,899 --> 00:03:10,938
The HiRISE camera on the Mars Reconnaissance
Orbiter can spot the rover itself,

55
00:03:11,182 --> 00:03:13,376
this dark dot, from space.

56
00:03:13,759 --> 00:03:15,942
But it could not resolve the stones.

57
00:03:16,267 --> 00:03:18,810
To see them, you had to drive into them.

58
00:03:19,321 --> 00:03:21,863
The channel is called Gediz Vallis.

59
00:03:22,246 --> 00:03:25,637
It winds down the side
of Mount Sharp, and it formed late,

60
00:03:25,811 --> 00:03:28,017
when Mars was already drying out.

61
00:03:28,458 --> 00:03:32,243
Floods of water and debris,
flows of mud and boulders,

62
00:03:32,301 --> 00:03:35,214
and dry avalanches all
left their marks here.

63
00:03:35,725 --> 00:03:39,777
Water also bleached pale halos
into some of the rocks.

64
00:03:40,288 --> 00:03:45,164
One scientist on the team summed it up:
this was not a quiet period on Mars.

65
00:03:45,489 --> 00:03:48,844
The sulfur stones were too small
and too brittle to drill.

66
00:03:49,169 --> 00:03:54,185
So engineers picked a large rock
nearby, nicknamed Mammoth Lakes,

67
00:03:54,335 --> 00:03:58,271
and found a place where the rover
could park on loose, sloping ground.

68
00:03:58,875 --> 00:04:04,076
On June eighteenth, Curiosity drilled
the forty-first sample hole of its mission.

69
00:04:04,459 --> 00:04:08,395
In this panorama, its arm
is still raised after drilling.

70
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:12,482
The rock powder went into small
laboratories inside the rover.

71
00:04:12,992 --> 00:04:15,616
Curiosity spent months in this channel.

72
00:04:16,058 --> 00:04:18,577
On October eleventh, twenty twenty-four,

73
00:04:18,856 --> 00:04:22,443
it took one last look
at the sulfur field and drove on.

74
00:04:22,884 --> 00:04:25,043
The question stayed behind.

75
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:31,278
In August twenty twenty-six, the team published
its analysis in the journal Science.

76
00:04:31,603 --> 00:04:35,225
Their main result:
these stones are native sulfur,

77
00:04:35,353 --> 00:04:38,243
and they appear to have formed
right where they sit.

78
00:04:38,568 --> 00:04:41,227
They were not washed
in from somewhere else.

79
00:04:41,610 --> 00:04:45,569
The team estimates they formed
roughly three billion years ago.

80
00:04:45,952 --> 00:04:49,517
Their proposed explanation
starts deep underground.

81
00:04:50,027 --> 00:04:52,779
Magma released sulfur-rich gases.

82
00:04:53,104 --> 00:04:58,026
The gases rose and cooled in frozen
ground near the surface, and,

83
00:04:58,305 --> 00:05:02,148
likely with help from water,
turned into pure sulfur.

84
00:05:02,844 --> 00:05:06,943
Much later, as the valley eroded,
the deposit was exposed.

85
00:05:07,326 --> 00:05:10,182
That is a proposal, not a closed case.

86
00:05:10,693 --> 00:05:13,456
Another group has suggested
something quite different.

87
00:05:14,268 --> 00:05:20,097
In their idea, a meteorite impact upstream
melted a sulfur-rich layer of rock,

88
00:05:20,375 --> 00:05:25,147
and liquid sulfur flowed about four
kilometers down the valley before it froze.

89
00:05:25,530 --> 00:05:29,187
But that idea depends on a rock
layer that nobody has measured yet.

90
00:05:30,139 --> 00:05:34,180
One thing this discovery
is not: evidence of life.

91
00:05:34,876 --> 00:05:38,684
The team says it sees no sign
that microbes played any role.

92
00:05:39,288 --> 00:05:43,944
What the stones do show, the authors
argue, is that ancient Mars had a way

93
00:05:43,978 --> 00:05:48,669
to concentrate sulfur, driven
by fluids rising from below.

94
00:05:49,052 --> 00:05:53,336
And they seem to have sat almost
untouched for billions of years.

95
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:57,504
One of the researchers called
Mars a frozen snapshot.

96
00:05:57,713 --> 00:05:59,536
So here is where it stands.

97
00:05:59,538 --> 00:06:03,474
We know what the stones are, where they
are, and roughly how old they are.

98
00:06:03,752 --> 00:06:05,877
We don't know for sure how they got there.

99
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:10,370
Part of the answer may sit in rock
layers upstream that Curiosity

100
00:06:10,428 --> 00:06:11,659
has not reached yet.

101
00:06:12,170 --> 00:06:16,581
Not bad, for a rock the rover
only found because it ran it over.
